//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// A memory space that declares `node: N` reaches the backend as a number the
// backend can act on.
//
// Every other property of a space is checked against the program and then
// described to the backend. This one has to be OBEYED: `mbind` takes a node
// number, and there is nowhere else for it to come from. A space is otherwise
// identified by an FNV hash of its name, and a hash cannot be turned back into
// a node -- so a space declaring a node is dispatched by a banded id instead,
// `arch::NUMA_DISPATCH_BASE + N`.
//
// That is what these checks pin: node 0 becomes 1000 and node 1 becomes 1001,
// carried on the `vx.transfer` the backend reads. The `space =` attribute is
// checked alongside so a renumbering cannot quietly swap which node a space
// gets -- the pair is the assertion, not either line alone.
//
// Both codegen paths, because they compute the id in different places: the AST
// path in lower/tensors.rs and the flat path in flat/emit/parallel.rs, from a
// `SubspaceInfo` built out of the same declaration. Two implementations of one
// rule is exactly the shape that drifts.
//
// REQUIRES: flat-codegen
// RUN: vxc %s --host default --machine fleet/xeon-8275cl.vx --action emit-mlir -o /dev/null 2>&1 | FileCheck %s
// RUN: vxc %s --legacy-codegen --host default --machine fleet/xeon-8275cl.vx --action emit-mlir -o /dev/null 2>&1 | FileCheck %s
// CHECK: vx.transfer
// CHECK-SAME: target_topology = 1000
// CHECK-SAME: space = "HBM"
// CHECK: vx.transfer
// CHECK-SAME: target_topology = 1001
// CHECK-SAME: space = "PEER_HBM"

fn main() -> i32 {
  let t : Tensor<f32, [64, 64]> = unsafe { Tensor<f32, [64, 64]>::uninit() };
  let near = transfer(t, Memory::HBM);
  let far = transfer(near, Memory::PEER_HBM);
  return 0;
}
